# Evidence is bytes, and git must not rewrite it.
#
# Captured records pin the SHA-256 of every artifact beside them, and the resolver's oracle
# gate and `tools/oracle/verify.py` re-hash those files from disk. A checkout that translated
# line endings would change the bytes without changing the repository, so the digest check
# would report "edited after capture" on one platform and pass on another — a false failure
# that says the evidence was tampered with when the checkout was.
#
# This is not a Windows workaround. A recorded digest is a claim about exact bytes; a working
# tree whose bytes depend on the host makes that claim unverifiable in both directions, and
# the same rule would apply if the translation ran the other way.
#
# `-text` rather than `binary`, so these stay diffable in review. They are meant to be read.

docs/spikes/oracle-records/** -text
docs/conformance/** -text

# Fixture corpora are parsed byte-exactly, and their hashes are recorded in the manifests of
# the runs that produced them. A CRLF checkout is a different corpus.
fixtures/** -text
